DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmqquuaanntt reads a PNM image as input.  It chooses _n_c_o_l_o_r_s colors to best
       represent  the  image,  maps  the  existing colors to the new ones, and
       writes a PNM image as output.

       This program is simply a combination of ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp and ppnnmmrreemmaapp, where
       the  colors of the input are remapped using a color map which is gener‐
       ated from the colors in that same input.  The  options  have  the  same
       meaning  as  in  those programs.  See their documentation to understand
       ppnnmmqquuaanntt.

       You may actually get fewer than nnccoolloorrss colors in the output because
         the method ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp uses to choose the best set of colors for the
         image is not the same as the method ppnnmmrreemmaapp uses to determine the
         best color from the set to represent an individual color.  For  exam‐
       ple,
         ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp may include salmon in the color map as the best
         representative  of a pink pixel in the input and include coral in the
       color
         map as the best representative of an actual coral pixel in the input.
       But
         ppnnmmrreemmaapp is free to use any color in the color map to represent that
         pink  pixel  and  would  find  coral  is a closer match for pink than
       salmon and
         therefore use coral for pink.  ppnnmmrreemmaapp might not use salmon
         for _a_n_y pixel.

       This waste of a slot in the color map is a consequence of the  approxi‐
       mate
         method ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp uses in order to compute the color map with a
         practical amount of computation.



   RRuunnnniinngg ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp aanndd ppnnmmrreemmaapp SSeeppaarraatteellyy
       It is much faster to call ppnnmmccoolloorrmmaapp and ppnnmmrreemmaapp directly than to run
       ppnnmmqquuaanntt.  You save the overhead of the Perl interpreter  and  creating
       two extra processes.  ppnnmmqquuaanntt is just a convenience.

       Here is an example of the relationship between the programs:

       This:

           $ pnmquant 256 myimage.pnm >/tmp/colormap.pnm >myimage256.pnm


       does essentially this:

           $ pnmcolormap 256 myimage.pnm >/tmp/colormap.pnm
           $ pnmremap -mapfile=/tmp/colormap.pnm myimage.pnm >myimage256.pnm



